WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.606
A leak often occurs when the last usable
pointer to an allocation is overwritten or

2
00:00:04.686 --> 00:00:04.983
lost.

3
00:00:05.063 --> 00:00:08.753
Once the program no longer retains a
pointer that can be used to release the

4
00:00:08.833 --> 00:00:11.927
allocation, the memory remains reserved
until process exit.

5
00:00:12.007 --> 00:00:14.506
The allocator may still know about it
internally,

6
00:00:14.586 --> 00:00:16.788
but the program has lost its cleanup
handle.

7
00:00:16.868 --> 00:00:20.973
Reassigning a pointer before freeing the
old allocation is a common way to abandon

8
00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:21.470
memory.

9
00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.120
A variable that once held the only owner
pointer is assigned a new value,

10
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.688
and the old value disappears.

11
00:00:26.768 --> 00:00:30.457
The safer pattern is to decide whether the
old allocation should be freed,

12
00:00:30.537 --> 00:00:34.762
transferred, stored elsewhere, or
intentionally retained before overwriting the

13
00:00:34.842 --> 00:00:35.615
owner pointer.

14
00:00:35.695 --> 00:00:39.206
Early returns can skip cleanup unless they
are handled deliberately.

15
00:00:39.286 --> 00:00:42.618
A function may allocate a buffer,
encounter a validation failure,

16
00:00:42.698 --> 00:00:44.960
and return before reaching the release
step.

17
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.669
This is especially common when several
error checks are added over time.

18
00:00:48.749 --> 00:00:53.034
Each early exit should either happen
before allocation or route through cleanup that

19
00:00:53.114 --> 00:00:54.720
knows which resources are live.

20
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.144
Partially initialized objects can leak if
cleanup only handles the fully initialized

21
00:00:59.224 --> 00:00:59.521
case.

22
00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:02.160
A structure may allocate several fields
one by one.

23
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.620
If allocation or initialization fails
midway,

24
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.985
cleanup must release the fields that
succeeded while avoiding fields that were never

25
00:01:09.065 --> 00:01:09.779
initialized.

26
00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:13.250
Initialization state should be clear
enough for cleanup to be safe.

27
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:17.496
Global caches, lists, and tables can hide
lost ownership when entries are replaced

28
00:01:17.576 --> 00:01:18.171
or removed.

29
00:01:18.251 --> 00:01:21.345
Replacing a value in a map may require
releasing the old value.

30
00:01:21.425 --> 00:01:25.352
Removing a list entry may or may not
release the pointed-to object depending on

31
00:01:25.432 --> 00:01:26.325
ownership rules.

32
00:01:26.405 --> 00:01:27.892
Those rules must be explicit.

33
00:01:27.972 --> 00:01:31.186
Code should make it easy to trace each
allocation to its release.

34
00:01:31.266 --> 00:01:34.717
During review, follow the pointer from
allocation through storage,

35
00:01:34.797 --> 00:01:37.534
transfer, replacement, error handling, and
cleanup.

36
00:01:37.614 --> 00:01:41.482
If that trace becomes vague, the code
needs a clearer owner, a cleanup helper,

37
00:01:41.562 --> 00:01:44.180
or a structure that keeps ownership state
together.
